{"name":"Croquet Blue","hex":"#4971ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"croquet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A game played on a lawn, in which players use mallets to drive balls through hoops (wickets).","A shot in this game in which the striker's ball and another ball are moved by hitting the striker's ball when they have been placed in contact following a roquet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To play a shot in the game of croquet in which the striker's ball and another ball are moved by hitting the striker's ball when they have been placed in contact following a roquet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/croquet"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Croquet","blocks":[{"text":"Croquet is a sport which involves hitting wooden, plastic, or composite balls with a mallet through hoops embedded in a grass playing court."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Modern_croquet_equipment.JPG/330px-Modern_croquet_equipment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croquet","wikidata":"Q193387"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Wycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg/500px-Wycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Wycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg/1280px-Wycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Wycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg/500px-Wycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leon Wyczółkowski, A Game of Croquet (1892–1895), National Museum, Warsaw","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWycz%C3%B3%C5%82kowski_Game_of_croquet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Croquetplayers.jpg/500px-Croquetplayers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Croquetplayers.jpg/1280px-Croquetplayers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Croquetplayers.jpg/500px-Croquetplayers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Croquet being played at a club in the UK; four balls are visible o